**No CSS framework is involved — the responsive behaviour is ~10 lines of hand-written vanilla CSS in `sanic/pages/styles/BasePage.css`, injected into every built-in page by a metaclass.**

### Where the viewport-dependent styling actually lives

`sanic/pages/styles/BasePage.css` (2.8 KB) is the whole "responsive layer". At commit `57d44f2` its viewport rules are:

```css
body > * { padding: 1rem 2vw; }

@media (max-width: 1000px) {
    body > * { padding: 0.5rem 1.5vw; }
    html {
        /* Scale everything by rem of 6px-16px by viewport width */
        font-size: calc(6px + 10 * 100vw / 1000);
    }
}
```

That `calc()` on the root `font-size` is the actual mobile adaptation strategy: every size on these pages is expressed in `rem`, so shrinking the root font proportionally to `100vw` below 1000 px scales the entire page down instead of reflowing it. Padding switches to `vw` units at the same breakpoint. (The rest of the file is `:root` custom properties — `--sanic-blue`, `--sanic-yellow`, etc. — plus a `prefers-color-scheme: dark` block.)

### How it reaches the error pages and directory listing

1. `sanic/pages/css.py` — `class CSS(ABCMeta)`: on class creation it resolves a stylesheet (explicit `STYLE_FILE`, else `styles/<ClassName>.css` next to the module), appends `STYLE_APPEND`, then walks `Page.__mro__` in reverse and concatenates each base's `STYLE` into `Page.CSS`. That's the cascade: BasePage's rules come first, the subclass's after.
2. `sanic/pages/base.py` — `class BasePage(ABC, metaclass=CSS)`, `style` property returns `self.CSS`, and `_head()` calls `self.doc.style(HTML(self.style))`. So the CSS is inlined into a `<style>` element in the head — no `<link>`, no external asset, no bundled framework.
3. `sanic/pages/error.py` (`ErrorPage`) and `sanic/pages/directory_page.py` (`DirectoryPage`) both subclass `BasePage`, so the block above applies to both.

Neither subclass adds viewport rules of its own: `ErrorPage.css` (2.1 KB) only constrains `#enduser { max-width: 30em }` and lays out the traceback with a `grid-template-columns: 1fr 5fr` key/value table; `DirectoryPage.css` (0.9 KB) styles the breadcrumbs and turns `table.autoindex tr` into a `display: flex` row — its only media query is `@media (prefers-color-scheme: dark)` for hover colour.

### One caveat worth flagging

`BasePage._head()` builds the `Document` with just `title`/`lang="en"`/`id="sanic"` — it never emits `<meta name="viewport" content="width=device-width, initial-scale=1">`. Media queries still fire (against the browser's default ~980 px layout viewport), so the `max-width: 1000px` block is effectively reached on most phones anyway, but the result is an unscaled 980 px layout rather than a true device-width layout. If the question behind the question is "how do I make these pages look right on mobile", that missing meta tag is the gap, not a framework.
